  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We have a horizontal tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ 2\\theta  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only two belong to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our curve has horizontal tangents at ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14267441/assets/bdbfbf6aae9a5f3e54aee1965a938815589a4e8b064670a6c587a3b779464f03.png)",
